{
  "analysis": "The user reports they can't curl example.com. I need to diagnose the issue. Let me start by reproducing the problem and gathering network diagnostics (DNS resolution, curl behavior, /etc/resolv.conf, /etc/hosts, firewall, etc.).",
  "plan": "First, attempt to curl example.com to see the actual error. Then check DNS resolution with getent/nslookup/dig, inspect /etc/resolv.conf and /etc/hosts, and check for any proxy environment variables that might interfere. Based on findings, I'll determine the root cause and fix it.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -v example.com\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "curl fails with 'DNS resolution error', which means DNS resolution is failing for example.com. I need to inspect the DNS configuration to find the root cause.",
  "plan": "Check /etc/resolv.conf for nameserver configuration, /etc/hosts, and environment proxy variables. Then test DNS resolution directly. I'll inspect the resolv.conf first since DNS resolution errors almost always point there.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/resolv.conf\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/hosts\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "env | grep -i proxy\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "resolv.conf uses Docker's embedded DNS resolver at 127.0.0.11, which forwards to the external servers listed (9.218.233.130, 9.218.230.76, 10.123.120.110). No proxy is set. The DNS resolution error could be because curl isn't actually getting a response, or there's a deeper resolver issue. Let me test resolution directly with other tools to isolate the cause.",
  "plan": "Run `getent hosts example.com` and `nslookup example.com 127.0.0.11` and `dig` to see whether the resolver itself works. Also test with a public resolver directly to understand whether it's the forwarders or the local resolver. This will tell me whether the fix is to point resolv.conf at a working nameserver or restart the resolver.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "getent hosts example.com; echo \"exit=$?\"\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "nslookup example.com 127.0.0.11\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Interesting — `getent hosts example.com` actually resolves successfully (to IPv6 AAAA addresses), but `curl` reports 'DNS resolution error'. This suggests curl is doing its own resolution differently, possibly only requesting A (IPv4) records and failing, or there's something specific about how curl resolves. Let me dig deeper into curl's behavior and what record types resolve.",
  "plan": "Test curl with -4 and -6 to see which address family fails. Check /etc/nsswitch.conf. Test resolution of A vs AAAA records. This will reveal whether it's an IPv4-only resolution failure or a curl-specific resolver issue.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -4 -v example.com\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -6 -v example.com\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: `getent hosts example.com` resolves successfully to IPv6 addresses, and there is no proxy. But `curl` (both -4 and -6) fails with a 'DNS resolution error'. This is unusual: the system resolver works, yet curl fails. This points to curl itself behaving differently — it may be built with the c-ares resolver, have a custom curlrc/alias, or be configured with a specific DNS. I need to inspect curl's configuration and resolver type.",
  "plan": "Check `curl --version` (to see if it uses c-ares or other resolver), check for aliases/functions (`type curl`), and look for curlrc files (/etc/curlrc, ~/.curlrc). Also confirm whether A (IPv4) records resolve to narrow down the issue. This will reveal the root cause.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl --version\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "type curl; alias 2>/dev/null | grep -i curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/curlrc 2>/dev/null; cat ~/.curlrc 2>/dev/null; echo '---'\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "getent ahosts example.com; echo \"exit=$?\"\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Critical clue: `curl --version` itself outputs 'DNS resolution error' — a version query does NO DNS resolution. This means /usr/bin/curl is not a genuine curl binary; it has been replaced or wrapped by something that prints that error regardless of arguments. Meanwhile the system resolver is fine (getent resolves A and AAAA records). So the root cause is a corrupted/replaced curl binary, not DNS. I need to inspect what /usr/bin/curl actually is.",
  "plan": "Inspect /usr/bin/curl: check file type, size, and contents. If it's a script/wrapper, read it to see why it always errors. Then determine the correct fix (restore real curl via package manager, or remove/replace the bad binary).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /usr/bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "file /usr/bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 300 /usr/bin/curl; echo; echo '---END---'\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Found the root cause. /usr/bin/curl is not a real binary — it's a 46-byte shell script that simply does `echo \"DNS resolution error\"` and `exit 11`. That's why even `curl --version` fails with that message (a version query doesn't do DNS). The actual network/DNS stack is perfectly fine: `getent` resolves example.com to both A and AAAA records, and curl's resolution error is a red herring produced by the bogus script. The fix is to restore the genuine curl binary.",
  "plan": "First identify the distro/package manager. Then reinstall curl to overwrite the fake script. I'll check available package managers (apt, apk, etc.). If package install isn't feasible, I'll restore via the appropriate means. Let me check the OS and try to reinstall curl.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/os-release 2>/dev/null | head -3; which apt apt-get apk yum dnf tce 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Root cause confirmed: /usr/bin/curl is a 46-byte fake shell script that unconditionally prints 'DNS resolution error' and exits 11. DNS is actually working fine (`getent` resolves example.com to A and AAAA records). The fix is to restore the real curl binary. The system is Ubuntu 24.04 with apt available, so I'll reinstall the curl package to overwrite the bogus script.",
  "plan": "Reinstall curl via apt to restore the genuine binary. The fake script will be replaced. I'll run `apt-get update` (brief) then `apt-get install --reinstall -y curl`. Then verify with `curl --version` and a real `curl example.com` request.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get install --reinstall -y curl\n",
      "duration": 30.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The reinstall failed because /var/cache/apt/archives has insufficient disk space. The real curl package isn't actually installed (the fake /usr/bin/curl is just a stray file, not from a package). I need to free up disk space before apt can download and install real curl. Let me check disk usage and clean apt's cache.",
  "plan": "Check filesystem usage with `df -h` and `apt-get clean` to clear old archives. If the partition is still too full, find large junk files to remove. Then retry the reinstall of curl. The genuine fix remains restoring real curl since DNS is fine and the fake script is the root cause.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h /\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get clean\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /var/cache/apt/archives 2>/dev/null; df -h /\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The root filesystem is at 100% capacity (0 bytes available), which blocked the apt download. apt-get clean only freed 8K. I need to find what's consuming the space and free enough to install real curl. The fake /usr/bin/curl is the root cause of the curl failure; DNS itself is fine. Once I free space, reinstalling curl will restore the real binary.",
  "plan": "Identify large consumers of disk space with `du` on likely directories (e.g., /var, /tmp, /root, /app). Then remove safe-to-delete bulk: apt lists cache, large log/temp files, or leftover archives. After freeing space, retry the curl reinstall. I'll be careful not to delete needed system files.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /var /tmp /root /app /var/log /var/lib 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The directories I checked are small (57M total), yet the filesystem reports 100% full with 962GB used. The space is likely consumed elsewhere (e.g., within the overlay, large files, or the apt lists). I need to locate the large consumers before I can install real curl. The root cause of the curl failure is still the fake /usr/bin/curl script; DNS works fine.",
  "plan": "Search for the largest files/directories on the system to find what's eating space. I'll use `du` on broad paths and `find` for large files. Once identified, I'll remove safe bulk (e.g., apt cached lists, old logs) to free enough space, then reinstall curl.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

